"""Create static course demonstration from computed temporal experiment tables."""
from pathlib import Path
import pandas as pd
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
s=pd.read_csv(ROOT/'results'/'temporal_haslemere_summary.csv')
c=pd.read_csv(ROOT/'results'/'temporal_haslemere_contrasts.csv')
d=pd.read_csv(ROOT/'results'/'temporal_risk_diagnostics.csv')
full=s[s.visibility==1].set_index('policy')
def pct(pol):return f'{100*full.loc[pol,"attack"]:.2f}%'
policy_labels={'none':'No testing','random':'Random','degree':'Observed recent degree','contact':'Recent confirmed contacts','adaptive':'Original adaptive heuristic','opt_filter':'Mean-field optimizer','opt_temporal_fitted':'Fitted temporal optimizer','opt_cumulative_fitted':'Fitted cumulative-history optimizer'}
trs=''.join(f'<tr><td>{lab}</td><td>{pct(key)}</td><td>{float(full.loc[key,"new_infections"]):.1f}</td></tr>' for key,lab in policy_labels.items())
h=d[(d.split=='heldout')&(d.method=='fitted')].iloc[0]
contrast=c[(c.visibility==1)&(c.contrast=='temporal fitted - opt_cumulative_fitted')].iloc[0]
html=f'''<!DOCTYPE html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>STAT 236 | Temporal network testing</title>
<style>body{{margin:0;color:#213348;background:#f5f7fb;font:16px/1.6 system-ui,sans-serif}}header{{background:#142b44;color:white;padding:54px 6vw}}header h1{{max-width:950px;font-size:clamp(2rem,4vw,3rem);line-height:1.1}}header p{{max-width:850px;color:#d5e2ee}}main{{max-width:1040px;margin:32px auto;padding:0 24px 65px}}a{{color:#136e88}}header a{{color:#8be4d9}}.cards{{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(200px,1fr));gap:16px}}.card,figure,section.panel{{background:white;padding:20px;border:1px solid #d9e0e9;border-radius:12px}}.num{{font-size:2rem;font-weight:750;color:#155576}}.sub{{color:#536579;font-size:14px}}h2{{margin-top:42px}}figure{{margin:18px 0}}img{{width:100%;height:auto}}figcaption{{color:#586b7c;font-size:14px}}table{{border-collapse:collapse;background:white;width:100%}}th,td{{padding:10px;border-bottom:1px solid #e0e6ed;text-align:left}}th{{background:#eaf1f5}}.grid{{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(300px,1fr));gap:15px}}.note{{padding:15px 18px;background:#fff7e9;border-left:4px solid #b78232}}footer{{margin-top:45px;font-size:14px;color:#65788a}}</style></head><body>
<header><p>STAT 236 · Fall 2026 · Project demonstration</p><h1>Network-aware testing on dynamic contact networks</h1><p>Do time-ordered proximity measurements improve resource-constrained testing when hidden infections and incomplete network information create uncertainty?</p><p><a href="report.pdf">Research report</a> · <a href="analysis.Rmd">Analysis notebook</a> · <a href="results/temporal_haslemere_replicates.csv">Replicate data</a></p></header>
<main><div class="cards"><div class="card"><div class="num">576</div><div class="sub">Measured five-minute Haslemere contact slots</div></div><div class="card"><div class="num">469</div><div class="sub">Participants; measured proximity, simulated infections</div></div><div class="card"><div class="num">20/day</div><div class="sub">Five tests in each of four daily decision rounds</div></div><div class="card"><div class="num">15</div><div class="sub">Paired epidemic replications in the new temporal experiment</div></div></div>
<h2>Two complementary network questions</h2><div class="grid"><section class="panel"><h3>SBM: structural variation</h3><p>A static network generates 50-day epidemics. Six community-strength ratios and five missing-edge levels test whether network structure alters the value of fitted-risk screening. Thirty paired replications per condition.</p></section><section class="panel"><h3>Haslemere: temporal contacts</h3><p>The measured graph changes every five minutes over three recorded days. We compare using recent contact weights with past cumulative weights in testing allocation. Transmission uses the actual sequence.</p></section></div>
<h2>Three-day Haslemere results</h2><p>These are outcomes of <strong>simulated infections on actual measured contacts</strong>, not observed COVID-19 cases. The total ever infected includes four people already confirmed at the start and 25 hidden initial infections.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Policy</th><th>Ever infected, complete observation</th><th>New infections in three days</th></tr></thead><tbody>{trs}</tbody></table>
<p class="note">The temporal fitted optimizer ({pct('opt_temporal_fitted')}) does not beat the causal cumulative-history optimizer ({pct('opt_cumulative_fitted')}) on average. Their paired difference is {contrast.diff_pp:+.2f} percentage points (95% Monte Carlo interval {contrast.ci_low:+.2f} to {contrast.ci_high:+.2f}). A richer time series is not automatically a better short-horizon testing signal.</p>
<div class="grid"><figure><img src="figures/temporal_haslemere_visibility.png" alt="Temporal policy curves under incomplete contact visibility"><figcaption>Retaining 25%, 50%, 75%, or all observed contact pairs; transmission always follows the complete measured temporal sequence.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="figures/optimized_community.png" alt="SBM community-strength interaction plot"><figcaption>Static SBM experiment: how fitted optimizer versus original heuristic varies across community strengths.</figcaption></figure></div>
<h2>Inference and optimization</h2><p>The state estimator is a factorized Bayesian filter over S/H/C/R disease states updated every five-minute slot. A logistic individual infection-risk model is fitted using <strong>60 independent simulated temporal epidemics</strong>, with 16 held-out epidemics. Held-out Brier score is {h.brier:.4f}; AUC is {h.auc:.3f}. A one-block additive expected-prevention objective selects five eligible people each round. The filter-only optimizer, the fitted optimizer, and the earlier adaptive heuristic remain distinct policies.</p>
<h2>Limits</h2><p>Only 7am–11pm contacts were recorded: we advance diagnosis and recovery overnight but do not model transmission on unobserved nighttime contacts. Epidemic parameters, initial infections, and testing responses are assumptions. Prior observed contacts inform decisions; no policy uses contacts from future recorded time slots. The cumulative-history baseline also uses only the observed prefix, not all three days in advance.</p>
<footer>Data: Kissler et al., Haslemere proximity measurements. Methods context: Firth et al. (2020); Huang, Ke and Jin (2022). <a href="README.md">Reproducibility instructions</a>.</footer></main></body></html>'''
(ROOT/'index.html').write_text(html)
print('Wrote',ROOT/'index.html')
